@juiceDeLemon for rounded corners, I just added this to my config:
```lua
        init = function()
            -- Add rounded corners.
            vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('User', {
                pattern = 'MiniFilesWindowOpen',
                callback = function(args)
                    local win_id = args.data.win_id
                    vim.api.nvim_win_set_config(win_id, { border = 'rounded' })
                end,
            })
        end,

```

> The `MiniFilesBufferCreate` event doesn't seem to be fired on any of the windows except the first when `preview = true`. I guess the preview window is creating the buffer but not firing the event, and then when you go in you're not creating a buffer so no event.

@IndianBoy42, thanks for the catch! I don't use `preview = true` myself, so didn't see that. I think I should use it for a while to try to catch some other unexpected behavior.

Your find should be fixed on `main`.

Also, I would like to suggest adding multiple mappings in the setup function, For example `go_in` for `l` and `<cr>`.
It's not critical but may help in transition from Luatree/NvimTree

```lua
mappings = {
  go_in = { '<CR>', 'l' }
}
```

> Also, I would like to suggest adding multiple mappings in the setup function, For example `go_in` for `l` and `<cr>`. It's not critical but may help in transition from Luatree/NvimTree
> 
> ```lua
> mappings = {
>   go_in = { '<CR>', 'l' }
> }
> ```

You can create your own buffer-local mapping. Here is [an example](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/f3648ab6ce4a5a44f632f98d5b6181a0463a5efc/doc/mini-files.txt#L406-L413).

In your case actual mapping command would be something like this: `vim.keymap.set('n', '<CR>', MiniFiles.go_in, { buffer = buf_id })`
